idealsComplex -- creates the Billera-Schenck-Stillman chain complex of ideals
*****************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        C = idealsComplex(V,F,r)
  * Inputs:
      * V, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, list of vertex coordinates of
        $\Delta$
      * F, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, list of facets of $\Delta$ (each
        facet is recorded as a list of indices of vertices taken from V)
      * r, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, integer, desired degree of
        smoothness
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * BaseRing (missing documentation) => a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,,
        default value null,
      * Homogenize (missing documentation) => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true,
      * CoefficientRing (missing documentation) => a *note ring:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, default value QQ,
      * VariableName (missing documentation) => a *note symbol:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value t,
  * Outputs:
      * C, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, Billera-Schenck-Stillman chain
        complex of ideals

Description
===========

This method returns the Billera-Schenck-Stillman chain complex of ideals whose
top homology is the module of non-trivial splines on $\Delta$.

+-------------------------------------+
|i1 : V = {{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{-1,-1}};|
+-------------------------------------+
|i2 : F = {{0,1,2},{0,2,3},{0,1,3}};  |
+-------------------------------------+
|i3 : C = idealsComplex(V,F,1);       |
+-------------------------------------+
|i4 : prune HH C                      |
|                                     |
|                 2                   |
|o4 = (QQ[t ..t ])                    |
|          0   2                      |
|                                     |
|     1                               |
|                                     |
|o4 : Complex                         |
+-------------------------------------+

The output from the above example shows that there is only one nonvanishing
homology, and it is free as a module over the polynomial ring in three
variables.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : V = {{-1,-1},{1,-1},{0,1},{-2,-2},{2,-2},{0,2}};                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : F = {{0,1,2},{0,1,3,4},{1,2,4,5},{0,2,3,5}};                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : C = idealsComplex(V,F,1);                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : prune HH C                                                              |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o8 = cokernel {2} | 8t_0 0        8t_1-2t_2 -2t_2    -2t_2    0        | <-- |
|              {2} | t_2  8t_0+t_2 t_2       8t_1+t_2 t_2      t_2      |     |
|              {2} | -t_2 -t_2     t_2       t_2      8t_1+t_2 8t_0-t_2 |     |
|                                                                             |
|     0                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o8 : Complex                                                                 |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|            3                                                                |
|(QQ[t ..t ])                                                                 |
|     0   2                                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|1                                                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The output from the above example shows that there are two nonvanishing
homologies, but the spline module, which is (almost) the homology HH_1, is
still free.  This shows that freeness of the spline module does not depend on
vanishing of lower homologies if the underlying complex is polyhedral.

See also
========

  * *note cellularComplex: cellularComplex, -- create the cellular chain
    complex whose homologies are the singular homologies of the complex
    $\Delta$ relative to its boundary
  * *note splineComplex: splineComplex, -- creates the Billera-Schenck-Stillman
    chain complex

